# Ecosistemas de Conocimiento — LOCAL DEMO ENVIRONMENT
# =============================================================================
#
#   cd infra && cp .env.example .env && docker compose up -d --wait
#
# This ONE file does TWO jobs, which is why it sits next to the compose file:
#   1. Docker Compose auto-loads `infra/.env` for `${VAR}` interpolation INSIDE
#      docker-compose.yml (ports, image limits, connection strings).
#   2. It is passed to backend / web / ai-service as `env_file`.
#
# ⚠ CONSEQUENCE OF (2): every variable below is visible inside those three app
#   containers, not just the one that needs it. Acceptable for a laptop demo
#   where all the values are fake. 🔴 NEVER acceptable in the fleet — there,
#   each app gets only its own variables (see infra/fleet/).
#
# ✅ THE THREE AUDIO CONTAINERS DELIBERATELY GET NO `env_file`. They receive an
#   explicit, minimal `environment:` block in docker-compose.yml instead. That
#   is on purpose: handing them this whole file would put ai-service's keys and
#   Payload's signing material inside a transcription worker for no reason, and
#   it is the fleet's per-app rule practised early where it is free.
#
# ⚠ Compose does NOT interpolate inside an env_file: a literal `${FOO}` is
#   passed through as the characters `${FOO}`. Avoid `$` in any value here.
#
# ─── LEGEND ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   🔑 SECRET   — a real credential in a real environment. Every value here is
#                 a placeholder. Rotate before anything is reachable off your
#                 laptop, and never commit a filled-in `.env`.
#   ⚙ CONFIG   — not secret, but wrong values break the stack.
#   💤 OPTIONAL — has a working default; set only to change behaviour.
#
# 🔴 ON THE PLACEHOLDER CREDENTIALS BELOW. They are deliberately weak, obvious
#    and identical for everyone who clones this repo. That is safe ONLY because
#    the demo binds to localhost. The moment any of this is reachable from a
#    network, every 🔑 value is a published credential. There is no "temporary"
#    exception to that.
# =============================================================================


# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 1. DATA TIER — one Postgres server, two databases; one Redis
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ⚙ Postgres superuser for the local cluster. Owns both databases.
POSTGRES_USER=edc
# 🔑 SECRET. Local placeholder. Compose REQUIRES this to be set — the stack
#    refuses to start without it rather than defaulting to something guessable.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=edc_local_dev_only

# ⚙ The two databases. Created on FIRST boot by infra/postgres/init/.
#    🔴 Separate databases, shared server, deliberately: Payload and Drizzle
#       both keep their migration ledger in schema `public` and would clobber
#       each other in a shared database.
BACKEND_DB_NAME=edc_backend
AI_DB_NAME=edc_ai

# ⚙ HOST-side ports. Offset from the defaults on purpose so the demo never
#    collides with a Postgres/Redis already installed on your machine. This
#    convention is inherited and worth keeping.
POSTGRES_HOST_PORT=55432
REDIS_HOST_PORT=56379
# ⚙ Object storage, same +50000 offset. 9000/9001 are NOT used deliberately —
#    they collide with php-fpm, Portainer, a developer's own MinIO and half of
#    Prometheus's ecosystem, which is the exact thing the offset exists to avoid.
RUSTFS_S3_HOST_PORT=59000       # ⚙ S3 API. ⚠ the BACKEND does NOT use this —
                                #   it reaches the store at http://rustfs:9000
RUSTFS_CONSOLE_HOST_PORT=59001  # ⚙ console. ✅ measured: the URL is
                                #   http://localhost:59001/rustfs/console/ —
                                #   the trailing path is REQUIRED. At `/` the
                                #   port answers with an S3 AccessDenied XML,
                                #   which looks broken and is not.


# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 2. HOST PORTS for the apps
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
WEB_HOST_PORT=3000           # ⚙ http://localhost:3000        — the product
BACKEND_HOST_PORT=3002       # ⚙ http://localhost:3002/admin  — Payload + IdP
AI_SERVICE_HOST_PORT=3001    # ⚙ http://localhost:3001/health — ai-service
AUDIO_HOST_PORT=3003         # ⚙ http://localhost:3003/health — audio-service
BGE_HOST_PORT=8001           # 💤 profile `heavy` only
RERANK_HOST_PORT=8002        # 💤 profile `heavy` only


# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 3. BACKEND — Next.js + Payload CMS (domain data AND identity provider)
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ⚙ 🔴 THE MOST IMPORTANT VARIABLE IN THIS FILE FOR THE DEMO.
#    EDC_ENV is the environment's NAME (local | dev | demo-0 | stage | prod).
#    `apps/backend/src/seed/env-gate.ts` boot-seeds ONLY for local and dev.
#    If the demo comes up with an empty database and no login, this is the
#    first thing to check — an empty database is the gate WORKING, not a bug.
#    ⚠ It is NOT NODE_ENV. NODE_ENV carries build semantics only and is
#      `production` even on the deployed dev box.
EDC_ENV=local

# 🔑 SECRET. Payload's own cookie/session secret. ⚠ NOT the token-signing key —
#    that is the AUTH_* block below, and confusing the two is easy.
#    Generate per environment: `openssl rand -hex 32`
PAYLOAD_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_local_demo_payload_secret_not_for_any_real_env

# ── 🔴 IDENTITY PROVIDER SIGNING KEYPAIR — the demo cannot mint tokens without
#       this, and NOTHING generates it for you. Run ONCE, then paste the output:
#
#           pnpm --filter @edc/backend keygen
#
#       It prints a PKCS#8 private key, an SPKI public key and a key id.
#       ⚠ Newlines inside a PEM must be escaped as \n to survive a dotenv file.
#       🔴 Generate a SEPARATE keypair per environment. A shared signing key
#          means a token minted on dev is valid in prod.
# AUTH_PRIVATE_KEY=   # 🔑 SECRET — PKCS#8 PEM, \n-escaped
# AUTH_PUBLIC_KEY=    # ⚙ SPKI PEM, published at /.well-known/jwks.json
# AUTH_KEY_ID=        # ⚙ the `kid`. Falls back to `identity-unversioned`
AUTH_ISSUER=http://localhost:3002   # ⚙ the `iss` claim. REQUIRED to issue at all.

# ── Bootstrap admin. Created on first boot IF the users table is empty. ──
# 🔴 `apps/backend/src/seed/admin.ts` has NO DEFAULT PASSWORD, by design — this
#    app is the identity provider, so a committed default would be a total
#    compromise, not a local inconvenience. Unset means "skip, loudly".
#    Recovery if it skips: Payload's own create-first-user flow at /admin.
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@ecosistemas.local
# 🔑 SECRET. Placeholder. This is the account you log in with at :3002/admin.
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=CambiaEstaClave_local_only

# 💤 Demo content (the demo organization, its teams, sample documents).
#    1 = on, 0 = off. Can only NARROW: it cannot turn seeding on where EDC_ENV
#    forbids it. That asymmetry is deliberate.
SEED_DOMAIN=1

# ⚙ CORS allowlist for the backend. The browser hits web on :3000 and the
#    backend directly on :3002, so both origins must be here.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:3002

# 💤 Identity/session tuning.
# AUTH_TOKEN_TTL=900                      # access-token lifetime, seconds
# IDENTITY_ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAIN=          # blank = any domain may register
# IDENTITY_RATELIMIT_TOKEN_USER_MAX=      # per-user token-mint cap per window

# ⚙ 🔴 Number of trusted reverse proxies in front of the container.
#    0 = trust nobody's X-Forwarded-For. CORRECT here (direct container access).
#    Setting this HIGHER than the real number of proxies is a silent, total
#    bypass of every per-IP rate limit — a caller then picks its own bucket key
#    and can also poison someone else's. In the fleet behind Traefik it is 1.
IDENTITY_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=0

# 🔑 SECRET. Static tokens other services present to the backend, in the
#    `x-service-token` header. JSON object: consumer slug -> secret.
#    ⚠ Malformed JSON means EVERY service-to-service call is rejected. It is
#      parsed at boot, so the failure is total and immediate rather than partial.
SERVICE_TOKENS={"web":"CHANGE_ME_local_web_service_token"}

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 🔴 THE SHARED CALLBACK SECRET — ONE VALUE, THREE CONSUMERS
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 🔑 SECRET. The HMAC key. `apps/backend` VERIFIES with it; `ai-service` and
#    `audio-service` SIGN with it. All three, the same value, deliberately:
#    `apps/backend/src/lib/hmac.ts` is ONE verifier reading ONE variable, and
#    both services emit the same `X-AIService-Timestamp` / `-Signature` /
#    `-Event` headers on purpose so no second verifier is needed.
#
# 🔴 THIS IS THE #1 THING TO GET RIGHT IN THE WHOLE STACK, because getting it
#    wrong produces NO ERROR ANYWHERE:
#      · ai-service mismatch   → uploads sit in `embedding` forever.
#      · audio-service mismatch → audio transcribes successfully, the callback
#        is rejected 401, and the document never becomes ready.
#    Both look like "it's still processing", indefinitely.
#
# ✅ SO IT IS WRITTEN EXACTLY ONCE, HERE. `docker-compose.yml` interpolates it
#    into ai-service's `API_KEYS` and into audio-service's `API_KEYS`, and
#    passes it to the backend through this file. The two JSON blobs are BUILT by
#    Compose, not pasted, so they physically cannot drift from this line.
#    ⚠ That is why there is no longer an `API_KEYS=` line in this file. If you
#      add one it will be IGNORED — `environment:` beats `env_file:` in Compose.
#
# Must be 32+ characters (audio-service `app/config.py` rejects a shorter one at
# boot). Generate a real one per environment: `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# 🔴 No `$` characters — this file's values are passed through literally.
CALLBACK_SECRET=local_demo_callback_secret_at_least_32_chars_long

# 🔑 SECRET. The bearer tokens the backend presents TO ai-service. Compose turns
#    these into the KEYS of ai-service's `API_KEYS` map.
AI_SERVICE_API_KEY=dev-key-app
AI_SERVICE_ADMIN_API_KEY=dev-key-admin

# 🔑 SECRET. The bearer tokens for audio-service. Same story: Compose turns
#    these into the KEYS of audio-service's `API_KEYS` map, with the `app` one
#    carrying `callbackSecret: ${CALLBACK_SECRET}`.
#    🔧 SEPARATE KEYS FROM ai-service ON PURPOSE — one key per consumer means a
#       leak is revocable without taking the other service down. It costs
#       nothing here and it is the habit worth having before the fleet.
AUDIO_SERVICE_API_KEY=dev-audio-key-app
AUDIO_SERVICE_ADMIN_API_KEY=dev-audio-key-admin

# 💤 If you ever need MORE than these two audio consumers, set the whole JSON
#    map here and change `API_KEYS:` on the three audio services in
#    docker-compose.yml to `${AUDIO_API_KEYS}`:
#      AUDIO_API_KEYS={"k1":{"org":"demo","project":"demo","role":"app","callbackSecret":"<must equal CALLBACK_SECRET>"},...}
#    🔴 The moment you do that, YOU own keeping `callbackSecret` byte-identical
#       to CALLBACK_SECRET above, and this file cannot help you — Compose does
#       NOT interpolate inside an env_file value. That trade is exactly why the
#       default path builds the JSON in compose instead.

# 💤 Session janitor — expires stale sessions. OFF by default.
# SESSION_JANITOR_ENABLED=1
# SESSION_TTL_DAYS=30
# SESSION_JANITOR_INTERVAL_MIN=1440
# SESSION_JANITOR_DRY_RUN=1        # 🔧 run this first before enabling for real

# 💤 Remaining per-IP / per-user rate limits. Defaults in parentheses.
# IDENTITY_RATELIMIT_TOKEN_IP_MAX=120
# IDENTITY_RATELIMIT_ME_USER_MAX=60
# IDENTITY_RATELIMIT_ME_IP_MAX=240
# IDENTITY_RATELIMIT_RESOLVE_CONSUMER_MAX=600
# IDENTITY_RATELIMIT_SERVICE_AUTH_FAIL_IP_MAX=20
# ⚠ These limiters are IN-PROCESS (a per-process Map), not Redis-backed. That is
#   fine on one container. 🔴 With two replicas each gets its own budget, so the
#   effective limit doubles and silently stops meaning what it says. The
#   Redis-backed version existed in the app this was merged from and was
#   deliberately dropped — see apps/backend/src/lib/rateLimit.ts.

# ⚠ PUBLIC_BACKEND_URL is NOT set here. It is set container-internally in
#   docker-compose.yml to `http://backend:3002`, because its consumer is
#   ai-service calling BACK — a container, not a browser. Setting it to
#   localhost here would break ingest callbacks.


# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 4. WEB — SvelteKit (adapter-node)
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ⚠ Read via `$env/dynamic/private` = RUNTIME env. One image serves every
#   environment. 🔴 Never rename any of these to PUBLIC_* — that makes them
#   build-time-inlined AND shipped to the browser.
# AI_SERVICE_URL / BACKEND_URL are set container-internally in
# docker-compose.yml and should NOT be set here.

# ⚙ 🔴 Tenant slug the frontend operates as. MUST be `demo`.
#    The web app's own default is `edc`, but `apps/backend/src/seed/demo.ts`
#    creates an organization with slug **`demo`**. Leave this unset and the
#    frontend looks for a tenant that does not exist.
ORG_KEY=demo

# 💤 Where chat system prompts come from.
#    unset  = inline prompt (the default)
#    registry = pull from ai-service's prompt registry
# PROMPT_SOURCE=

# ⚠ BACKEND_URL is set container-internally in docker-compose.yml. Do NOT
#   set it here. Its code default is `http://localhost:3002`, which inside a
#   container means "this container" — so a forgotten value targets the web app
#   itself and fails in a confusing way rather than an obvious one.

# ⚠ 🔴 `ORIGIN` is not set anywhere and probably needs to be, in the FLEET.
#   SvelteKit's adapter-node rejects cross-site form POSTs unless it knows its
#   own public origin. This app uses form actions and cookies. Behind Traefik
#   set either `ORIGIN=https://<host>` or
#   `PROTOCOL_HEADER=x-forwarded-proto` + `HOST_HEADER=x-forwarded-host`.
#   ✅ Not needed locally — there is no proxy in front of the demo.


# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 5. AI-SERVICE — Fastify + Drizzle + BullMQ + pgvector
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# DATABASE_URL and REDIS_URL are set container-internally in docker-compose.yml.

# ⚠ `API_KEYS` IS DELIBERATELY NOT SET HERE ANY MORE.
#   It used to be a literal JSON blob with the callback secret pasted inside it,
#   i.e. the secret written twice, in two files, with nothing checking they
#   agree. `docker-compose.yml` now BUILDS that JSON from
#   `AI_SERVICE_API_KEY` + `AI_SERVICE_ADMIN_API_KEY` + `CALLBACK_SECRET`
#   (see §3), so the pair cannot drift. Setting `API_KEYS=` here would be
#   silently ignored: Compose gives `environment:` precedence over `env_file:`.

# ── 🔴 THE NO-CREDENTIALS DEMO PATH ──────────────────────────────────────────
# With these at 1, the stack boots and the whole UI is clickable with ZERO API
# keys and ZERO cost: the router and the embedder are deterministic stubs.
# What you get: the real product shape — upload, ingest, chat, citations,
#               1-5 star ratings, multitenancy.
# What you do NOT get: real answers. Retrieval is stub vectors, so relevance is
#               meaningless. Do not demo answer QUALITY in this mode.
# To demo real answers: set OPENROUTER_API_KEY, set all three to 0, restart.
MOCK_LLM=1
MOCK_EMBEDDINGS=1
MOCK_LLM_STREAM=1

# 🔑 SECRET. Platform-wide OpenRouter key. Only needed when the MOCK_* flags
#    above are 0. Required in production — ai-service ABORTS BOOT without it
#    when NODE_ENV=production, so the fleet manifests must always carry it.
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-REPLACE-ME

# 💤 Attribution header sent to OpenRouter.
# OPENROUTER_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# 💤 Embeddings seam. Defaults to OpenRouter. Repointing this at a self-hosted
#    box needs no code change — SAME WEIGHTS MEANS IDENTICAL VECTORS, so no
#    re-embed. Different weights means re-embedding your whole corpus.
# EMBEDDINGS_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/embeddings
# EMBEDDING_MODEL=baai/bge-m3
# 🔴 EMBEDDING_DIM must match the `chunks.embedding` vector(N) column. Changing
#    it is a MIGRATION, not a config change.
# EMBEDDING_DIM=1024

# ── Advanced RAG (compose `--profile heavy`). Both OFF by default. ──
# 🔴 The URLs are consumed VERBATIM — `/rerank` and `/embed` are part of the
#    value, not appended by the client. A missing path 404s, and the fallback
#    swallows it, so the feature looks enabled and silently does nothing.
#    The URLs themselves are set in docker-compose.yml; these are the switches.
RERANK_ENABLED=false
SPARSE_ENABLED=false
# 💤 RERANK_MODEL=baai/bge-reranker-v2-m3
# 💤 RERANK_TIMEOUT_MS=5000       # on timeout OR error, falls back, never fails
# 💤 SPARSE_DIM=250002            # pinned to bge-m3's vocab; mismatch aborts boot
# 💤 SPARSE_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# 💤 Timeouts. 0 disables each bound.
# CONDENSE_TIMEOUT_MS=5000        # query condensation; fails SAFE to raw question
# LLM_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=120000   # whole generation
# LLM_STREAM_STALL_TIMEOUT_MS=45000  # inactivity on a stream — the likelier hang

# 💤 LOG_LEVEL=info               # fatal|error|warn|info|debug|trace|silent


# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 6. AUDIO-SERVICE — upload -> Spanish transcript -> signed callback
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ✅ RECONCILED against the real service (2026-08-14): its README, Dockerfile,
#    .env.example and app/config.py. The earlier version of this section was
#    written before the service existed and named variables it does not read
#    (`WHISPER_MODEL_DIR`, `TRANSCRIBE_CONCURRENCY`). Those are gone.
#
# 🔴 IT IS THREE CONTAINERS, NOT ONE: the API, the transcription worker, and
#    `audio-service-callbacks`. Leaving out the callback worker breaks the
#    product with no error message anywhere — audio transcribes fine and no
#    document ever becomes ready. Full argument in docker-compose.yml.
#
# 🔑 Its bearer tokens are AUDIO_SERVICE_API_KEY / AUDIO_SERVICE_ADMIN_API_KEY
#    in §3, next to the CALLBACK_SECRET they are paired with. They live there
#    rather than here so the secret and its consumers are visible together.

# ⚙ 🔴 THE WEIGHTS. This ONE variable feeds BOTH the image build arg and the
#    container env, because they must agree: the weights are BAKED INTO THE
#    IMAGE and the runtime sets `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1`, so a container whose
#    WHISPER_MODEL differs from its build arg cannot download and dies at boot.
#    🔴 CHANGING THIS REQUIRES A REBUILD, not a restart:
#         docker compose build audio-service && docker compose up -d
#
#      small          ~250 MB  ← THE DEFAULT HERE. ~2-4x realtime on 4 cores.
#                              A 10-min recording is ~3-5 min. Good Spanish.
#      large-v3-turbo ~800 MB  the service's OWN default and better Spanish,
#                              but ~1x realtime: a 10-min recording takes
#                              ~10 min. Use it on the fleet, not on a laptop.
#      medium / base / tiny    the usual ladder. `tiny` is dev-iteration only.
#
#    ⚠ WE DEVIATE FROM THE SERVICE'S DEFAULT ON PURPOSE. Its README says
#      `small` is "the right pick for a laptop demo" and this file is the
#      laptop demo. The fleet manifests keep the better weights.
WHISPER_MODEL=small

# 💤 Used only by docker-compose.dev.yml. Also a BUILD ARG there — same rule.
WHISPER_MODEL_DEV=small

# ⚙ int8 on CPU. `float16` is the GPU value; see below.
WHISPER_COMPUTE_TYPE=int8

# ⚙ Pinned. An empty value forces autodetection, which mis-detects short Spanish
#    clips as Portuguese often enough to be worth avoiding.
WHISPER_LANGUAGE=es

# 💤 INFO | DEBUG | WARNING | ERROR | CRITICAL (Python levels, not pino's).
AUDIO_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# ⚙ 🔴 CPU CEILING FOR THE TRANSCRIPTION WORKER. This is the limit that keeps
#    the DEMO responsive, not the one that protects the worker. Without it,
#    transcription takes every core and the UI you are demoing gets sluggish —
#    which reads as a product bug in front of an audience.
#    It is applied to `audio-service-worker` only; the API and the callback
#    worker are cheap. `WHISPER_CPU_THREADS` is set equal to it in compose, so
#    CTranslate2 does not try to use host cores the cgroup will not give it.
#    Raise both on a bigger machine.
AUDIO_CPUS=4
AUDIO_MEMORY=4g

# ⚙ 🔴 CPU-ONLY, AND THAT IS THE POINT. The service is
#    built CPU-first. If a GPU ever appears, it is a
#    two-variable flip on the WORKER container only — `WHISPER_DEVICE=cuda`,
#    `WHISPER_COMPUTE_TYPE=float16` — plus `gpus: all` in compose. No code
#    change, no API change, no re-bake. Do not design anything around it.

# 💤 Everything else has a working default in `app/config.py` and is documented
#    in `services/audio-service/.env.example`. The ones most likely to matter:
# MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=536870912     # 512 MiB, enforced WHILE streaming to disk
# MAX_DURATION_SECONDS=21600     # 6 hours
# MAX_CONCURRENT_JOBS=1          # 🔴 leave at 1 on CPU; 2 is slower in total
# JOB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=14400      # per attempt; must exceed the worst case
# TRANSCRIPT_TTL_SECONDS=86400   # 🔴 after this the transcript is GONE — the
#                                #    signed callback is the durable handoff,
#                                #    and the consumer must persist on receipt


# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 7. ☁ OBJECT STORAGE — RustFS, S3-compatible. LOCAL AND DEV ONLY.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# WHY THIS SECTION CHANGED. It used to say "not used locally, uploads go to
# local disk". That was true and it stopped being good enough: users upload
# **audio**, which is 10–100× the size of a PDF. Local disk was already the
# single biggest constraint on fleet shape (one prod box, forever); audio is
# what made it a local problem too.
#
# 🔴 RustFS IS FOR LOCAL AND `dev`. NOT stage, NOT prod. That line is drawn on
#    purpose and the reason is in `infra/fleet/topology.md` §3 — 25 CVEs in 8
#    months, every release flagged `prerelease`, open issues for silent data
#    loss on single-drive. Fine for a laptop, defensible for `dev`, not for a
#    box holding a customer's only copy of their audio. The stage/prod engine
#    is an OPEN DECISION, not a to-do that was forgotten.
#
# ─── 🔴 THESE FIVE ARE READ FROM **THIS FILE**, NOT FROM docker-compose.yml ──
# That is deliberate, and it is the only way the fallback works. The backend's
# contract is: **when all five are ABSENT, fall back to local disk.** Compose's
# `environment:` block cannot express "absent" — `${S3_ENDPOINT:-}` sets the
# variable to the EMPTY STRING, which is a different thing and depends on how
# the backend's check happens to be written. Reaching the container through
# `env_file:` means commenting the line out here genuinely removes it.
#
# ✅ SO: TO TURN OBJECT STORAGE OFF, COMMENT OUT ALL FIVE LINES BELOW. Uploads
#    go back to the `backend_media` volume and nothing else changes. `rustfs`
#    still starts (`backend` depends on it) and idles at ~100 MB; if that
#    matters, `docker compose stop rustfs` afterwards.
#    🔴 ALL FIVE OR NONE. A partial set is the worst outcome — a store the app
#       half-believes in.

# ⚙ 🔴 CONTAINER-NETWORK ADDRESS. `rustfs` is the compose SERVICE NAME and 9000
#    is the CONTAINER port. ⚠ NOT `localhost:59000` — that is the host-side
#    mapping for YOUR browser and curl, and inside the backend container
#    `localhost` means the backend itself. This is the same trap as
#    BACKEND_URL in §4, and it fails confusingly rather than obviously.
S3_ENDPOINT=http://rustfs:9000

# ⚙ RustFS does not care about the region, but the AWS SDK REQUIRES one to sign
#    a request at all. `us-east-1` is the conventional value for a self-hosted
#    store. ✅ Verified: SigV4 with `us-east-1` is accepted by this image.
S3_REGION=us-east-1

# ⚙ 🔴 THE BUCKET IS NOT CREATED BY docker-compose.yml, AND RUSTFS CANNOT
#    CREATE IT FROM THE ENVIRONMENT. ✅ Verified against the running container:
#    the only variables it reads are RUSTFS_ACCESS_KEY / _SECRET_KEY /
#    _VOLUMES / _ADDRESS. There is no `RUSTFS_DEFAULT_BUCKET`. (Garage, the
#    engine the fleet doc names for self-hosted stage/prod, HAS one. RustFS
#    does not — do not carry that assumption across.)
#    So the bucket is created by the backend's provisioning step, which must be
#    IDEMPOTENT and run on EVERY boot — see the note under `rustfs_data` in
#    docker-compose.yml for why a one-shot init container is the wrong shape.
S3_BUCKET=edc

# 🔑 SECRET (placeholder, like everything else here). 🔴 THESE ARE ALSO THE
#    STORE'S **ROOT** CREDENTIALS. docker-compose.yml interpolates the same two
#    values into `RUSTFS_ACCESS_KEY` / `RUSTFS_SECRET_KEY`, exactly the way
#    CALLBACK_SECRET is handled in §3, so the store's root key and the app's key
#    physically cannot drift.
#    ⚠ WHY ROOT AND NOT A SCOPED USER: RustFS's IAM store is VOLUME-RESIDENT and
#      no environment variable seeds a user or a policy. A scoped user only
#      exists after provisioning has run against a live server; root is the only
#      identity that exists before that. Acceptable here because every credential
#      in this file is fake. 🔴 On `dev` in the fleet, issue a scoped user with
#      an explicit policy and keep root out of the app — `env-manifests.md` §5.
#    ⚠ These land in `users.json` on the `rustfs_data` volume IN CLEARTEXT. A
#      copy of that volume is a copy of every key it ever issued.
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=edclocal
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=edclocal_secret_not_for_any_real_env

# ⚠ NOT in the agreed five-variable contract, and NOT set here, but you will
#   need it in the CODE: `forcePathStyle: true` is mandatory for every
#   self-hosted S3 engine. Virtual-host style would require the store to answer
#   on `<bucket>.rustfs`, which resolves to nothing on the compose network.
#   It is a client setting, not an environment variable, so it belongs in the
#   backend's adapter config — owner: `apps/backend/**`.
# ⚠ And the other client-side landmine, from `topology.md` §3: **AWS SDK v3
#   ≥ 3.729.0 breaks presigned PUT on every S3-compatible store**, real AWS
#   included. One-line fix, in the client, not here:
#     requestChecksumCalculation: 'WHEN_REQUIRED'
#     responseChecksumValidation: 'WHEN_REQUIRED'

# 💤 Memory ceiling for the store. ⚠ RustFS open issue #2573: its memory use
#    climbs toward whatever ceiling it is given, where other stores held far
#    less. So this is the number it will GROW INTO, not a safety net that is
#    never touched. ✅ Measured idle on an empty store with no limit: 102 MiB.
#    🔧 Raise it if RustFS gets OOM-killed under a real upload load — and know
#       that an OOM here IS the #2573 symptom, not a mystery.
RUSTFS_MEMORY=1g

# ── Corpus language ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Postgres text-search config (`regconfig`) for corpora CREATED from now on —
# a config name, not a locale code: `spanish`, `english`, `simple`.
#
# ⚠ Drives the lexical half of hybrid retrieval. `simple` does NO stemming, so
#   `acuerdo` will not match `acuerdos`. That costs more here than in an English
#   product: Spanish is heavily inflected and half the corpus is transcribed
#   speech, where nobody repeats a term in the form someone later searches for.
#
# ⚠ APPLIED AT CREATION ONLY. Changing this does not re-configure a corpus that
#   already exists — those need `corpora.update` or a rebuild.
CORPUS_LANGUAGE=spanish
